      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[16^{x^2 + y} + 16^{x + y^2} = 1.\\]\nFirst, we express 16 as a power of 2:\n\\[16 = 2^4,\\] so\n\\[16^{x^2 + y} = (2^4)^{x^2 + y} = 2^{4(x^2 + y)}\\]\nand\n\\[16^{x + y^2} = (2^4)^{x + y^2} = 2^{4(x + y^2)}.\\]\nThus, the equation becomes\n\\[2^{4(x^2 + y)} + 2^{4(x + y^2)} = 1.\\]\nSince the left-hand side is a sum of two powers of 2, and the only way their sum can be 1 is if each power is 0 (because any positive power of 2 other than 0 would be greater than 1), we must have:\n\\[4(x^2 + y) = 0\\]\nand\n\\[4(x + y^2) = 0.\\]\nFrom \\(4(x^2 + y) = 0\\), we get:\n\\[x^2 + y = 0 \\implies y = -x^2.\\]\nFrom \\(4(x + y^2) = 0\\), we get:\n\\[x + y^2 = 0 \\implies y^2 = -x.\\]\nSubstituting \\(y = -x^2\\) into \\(y^2 = -x\\), we get:\n\\[(-x^2)^2 = -x \\implies x^4 = -x.\\]\nFactoring out \\(x\\) gives:\n\\[x(x^3 + 1) = 0.\\]\nThis equation has solutions:\n\\[x = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x^3 = -1 \\implies x = -1.\\]\nIf \\(x = 0\\), then \\(y = -0^2 = 0\\), so one solution is \\((0, 0)\\).\nIf \\(x = -1\\), then \\(y = -(-1)^2 = -1\\), so another solution is \\((-1, -1)\\).\nThus, the only solutions are \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((-1, -1)\\). Therefore, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
